ClassicLadder bit I/O through real HAL pins.

The ladder engine itself is covered exhaustively by Go tests in
src/gomc/internal/classicladder, including differential runs against the
LinuxCNC 2.9 engine. What those cannot reach is the layer between HAL and the
ladder, because they drive the engine directly: that in-NN reaches the ladder
variables, that out-NN carries back what the ladder computed, that the module
loads its .clp project, and that it exports its scan function under a name a
config can addf. All four were broken before the engine port, and only a
running HAL shows them.

estop.clp is a copy of the ladder from configs/sim/axis/classicladder — a real
project rather than a synthetic one, so the load path is exercised on something
a user would actually run. It is a copy and not a symlink because module
arguments are resolved and contained server-side (internal/pathres): a symlink
pointing outside the config directory is refused, as it should be.

The input sequence drives the estop chain: enable, reset strobe, the latch
holding after the strobe drops, an external estop trip, no auto-recovery when
the button is released, a second reset, and finally the enable going away.

`expected` is generated by gen-expected.sh from the 2.9 engine, so the
expectation is the original implementation's behaviour rather than our own
output frozen in place. Re-run it only when you intend the expectation to move.
